<h1><hr class="Journal2">ROAD TRIP TO DEVILS TOWER - Tuesday, August 17, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20040817/P1010030.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20040817/Small/P1010030.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20040817/P1010056.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20040817/Small/P1010056.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20040817/P1010041.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20040817/Small/P1010041.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20040817/P1010063.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20040817/Small/P1010063.JPG"></a>
<p>
After a really blustery night I wake to a gloomy, overcast day.  Good thing I'll just be driving today.  My destination?  Devils Tower National Monument in northeastern Wyoming.  On the way I pass through scenic and colorful Bighorn National Forest.
<p>
Sadly, just before sunset I encounter a dead fawn along the roadside.  Moved by the mishap, I pull over and drag the dead animal into the ditch, wanting to spare the sight from other drivers who will pass this way.  Soon thereafter I reach Devils Tower.  I drive around for a bit and eventually find a spot to van camp in a pasture located well off the main road.  I quickly settle in for the night.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20040817&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
